            <ArticleTitle>Alpha1 antitrypsin and the prevalence and severity of asthma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>230-1</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In a random sample of children (aged 9-11 years; n = 5629), who were studied according to the ISAAC phase II protocol, heterozygosity of the alpha1 antitrypsin (alpha1-AT) Pi genotypes MS or MZ, or low alpha1-AT plasma levels, were not associated with an increased risk of developing asthma. Asthmatics with low levels of alpha1-AT were particularly prone to develop airway hyperresponsiveness and reduced lung function.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
